  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Dehydration of IV is most facile since, it gives an aromatic compound. Dehydration of III gives a conjugated diene which is stabilized by resonance. <br/><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/06db46f3-8551-4385-9c97-2082a51514b0.png\" style=\"width:2.3in;height:0.74653in\"> <br/><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/c799289f-e824-4fbd-b568-7b2ed18114d1.png\" style=\"width:2.27986in;height:0.75972in\"> <br/>Dehydration of II gives only cyclohexene which is not stabilized by resonance. <br/><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/ab09ad99-087f-4f2c-8b56-a42a953ebb92.png\" style=\"width:2.25347in;height:0.75347in\"> <br/>In contrast, phenol (I) does not undergo dehydration. Thus, the ease of dehydration is <br/>IV &gt; III &gt; II &gt; I.",
    "image": null
  },
